title: LSP Server

!! Serving an editor from the running wiki

`--lsp` starts a Language Server Protocol server inside the TiddlyWiki process. It is read only: it answers questions about the wiki and never writes to it.

!!! Starting

```
tiddlywiki ./mywiki --lsp
```

The server listens on `127.0.0.1:6009`.

|! Parameter |! Default |! Meaning |
|`port=<n>` |6009 |TCP port to listen on |
|`host=<h>` |127.0.0.1 |Interface to bind |
|`stdio` |off |Serve over stdin and stdout instead of a socket. Cannot be combined with `--mcp`, see [[LSP and MCP]] |

!!! Completion

Typing `[[` or `{{` offers tiddler titles, narrowed as you keep typing. A title containing spaces completes correctly, because the server states the range to replace rather than leaving the editor to guess where the word began.

System tiddlers stay out of the list until the prefix starts with `$:/`, which also reaches shadow tiddlers.

Matching is by prefix and ignores case, and needs at least two characters. See [[LSP Capabilities]] for the exact rules.

!!! Diagnostics

A link or transclusion whose target does not exist is marked as a warning naming the missing title. Shadow tiddlers count as targets.

Left alone: external addresses, filtered transclusions (`{{{ ... }}}`), inline code, fenced code blocks, and the field header of a `.tid` file, where a `tags` field routinely names tiddlers that do not exist yet.

!!! Hover

Resting the cursor on a filter expression runs it against the live wiki and reports what it matches right now. A filter you have not finished typing says so rather than claiming to match nothing.

!!! Unsaved text

The server mirrors the buffers your editor has open, so all three features answer for text you have not saved.

